{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794339947055,"sessionID":"ses_e390f5f67ffeyzZwSqPLXNKUwZ","part":{"id":"prt_1c6fec213001N8FwbCoGvMtDpF","messageID":"msg_1c6fe882b0019d1QuFHSPDOizB","sessionID":"ses_e390f5f67ffeyzZwSqPLXNKUwZ","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1794339947056,"sessionID":"ses_e390f5f67ffeyzZwSqPLXNKUwZ","part":{"id":"prt_1c6fec21600104HAxEnvGlK7QA","messageID":"msg_1c6fe882b0019d1QuFHSPDOizB","sessionID":"ses_e390f5f67ffeyzZwSqPLXNKUwZ","type":"text","text":"<think>\"no such table: sessions\" — the schema wasn't created properly. The issue: I changed the SCHEMA string for client_attempts, and the `init_db()` uses `conn.executescript(SCHEMA)`. Hmm, but the tables don't exist at all?\n\nWait, \"no such table: sessions\" — but the register endpoint worked earlier. Hmm, the error occurs in `current_account` → the traceback line numbers look garbled (the source lines don't match the code — \"data = request.get_json(silent=True)\" inside current_account?). That's just gunicorn's traceback line mapping being off? No... the traceback shows weird line contents. That suggests the .pyc cache is stale or the file changed while the server was running? No, the server started fresh.\n\nHmm, \"no such table: sessions\" — but init_db() ran at import. Unless init_db() failed silently? No, it would raise at import.\n\nOH WAIT. I see: `rm -rf data` deleted the data directory while the server was starting? No, the rm happened before the server started.\n\nHmm, but the DB file is at ./data/app.db and DATA_DIR is created in init_db ✓.\n\nHmm, \"no such table: sessions\" — maybe the database file is different from the one init_db created? Note: `init_db()` uses `sqlite3.connect(DB_PATH)` and `get_db()` uses `sqlite3.connect(DB_PATH)` — the same path... unless the CWD differs! `DB_PATH = DATA_DIR / \"app.db\"` where DATA_DIR = Path(\"data\") — a RELATIVE path. If the CWD changes between connections, the DB path changes! But the CWD doesn't change...\n\nHmm, but wait: gunicorn's `--pythonpath backend` with the app imported... the CWD is /workspace ✓.\n\nLet me look at the actual traceback more carefully:\n```\nFile \"/workspace/backend/app.py\", line 396, in me\nFile \"/workspace/backend/app.py\", line 210, in require_account\nFile \"/workspace/backend/app.py\", line 202, in current_account\n    data = request.get_json(silent=True)\nFile \"/workspace/backend/app.py\", line 262, in rate_limit_retry_after\nsqlite3.OperationalError: no such table: sessions\n```\nThe source lines shown don't match the actual code at those line numbers → the .py file on disk changed AFTER the process loaded it! I edited app.py while the server was running? No... I edited it before starting the server.\n\nOH! I bet the issue is that the file was edited while the previous gunicorn (from an earlier test) was still running... no.\n\nHmm, the mismatched line numbers suggest the running code is from a DIFFERENT version of app.py than what's on disk now. Which means the server was started BEFORE my edits? No — I started it in this last command, after the edits.\n\nUnless... there's a stale __pycache__ for app.py? Python checks the mtime, so no.\n\nWait, actually — maybe the gunicorn log contains output from BOTH the previous run (19:38) and this run (19:45). The timestamps show 19:45:20 — that's this run. Hmm.\n\nLet me check the actual line numbers in the current file: line 396 in `me`? Let me look. And line 210 in require_account?\n\nActually, the mismatch might be because the file has different content than what gunicorn loaded — e.g., gunicorn's `--pythonpath backend` imports `app` from backend/. But is there another `app` module shadowing it? E.g., /workspace/app.py? No.\n\nHmm, hold on: maybe the issue is that the DB file was created but the tables weren't... Let me just check the DB directly.\n\nActually — I wonder: did `rm -rf data` happen while a PREVIOUS gunicorn (still running from an earlier test, e.g. the one I killed but maybe not) held the DB? Let me check: I killed $SRV at the end of the earlier test. But there might be a leftover gunicorn from the earlier failed background runs (pid 6631 was found). If a stale gunicorn (with the OLD code) was still running and holding port 8000, then my new gunicorn would fail to bind (address in use) and the tests would hit the OLD server!\n\nYES — that's it. The old server (started with the old code, before I added the client_attempts table) was still running and bound to port 8000. My new gunicorn couldn't bind (\"Address already in use\") and the tests hit the old server, whose DB file was deleted (rm -rf data) → the old process's cached... hmm, but the old process opens a new connection per request → the deleted file → a new empty DB → \"no such table\" ✓✓ That explains everything!\n\nSo: kill all gunicorn processes before starting, and check the log for bind errors.\n\nLet me redo the test with a clean start.</think>\n\n","time":{"start":1794339947030,"end":1794339947051}}}
